# Rust
rust/**/target/
rust/**/*.rs.bk
# Cargo.lock IS committed. This workspace ships binaries (trust-core,
# credential-vault, kill-switch, defstack-cli), where Cargo'''s own guidance is to
# commit it -- and the repository claims SLSA provenance, which a floating
# dependency resolution would quietly contradict. npm lockfiles were already
# committed; this removes the inconsistency.
# Only the workspace root has a lockfile. Nested ones are Cargo artifacts that the
# workspace ignores, and committing one invites edits to a file nothing reads.
#
# `rust/*/Cargo.lock` matched exactly one directory level, so rust/trust-core/fuzz/Cargo.lock
# slipped past it and was committed -- a second, unsynchronised pin of ed25519-dalek and
# curve25519-dalek governing the signature_decode fuzz target, with nothing keeping it aligned
# with the workspace. `**` catches it at any depth.
rust/**/Cargo.lock
!rust/Cargo.lock

# Python
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
python/**/.pytest_cache/
python/**/.ruff_cache/
python/**/.venv/
python/**/venv/
python/**/*.egg-info/
python/**/build/
python/**/dist/

# TypeScript / Node
typescript/**/node_modules/
# The desktop shell is not part of the typescript workspace: it is installed on its own so a
# ~150 MB Electron download is never part of the workspace install, and its policy tests need
# neither. Ignored here rather than by a broader pattern, so a stray node_modules elsewhere in
# the tree is still noticed.
desktop/node_modules/
# Installers, and the compiled `warrantor` binary the release workflow drops in for
# electron-builder to bundle. Neither belongs in git: a committed executable in the source tree of
# a security substrate is an unreviewed, unattested binary that nothing in CI ever looks at, and
# the whole point of building it in the release job is that the artifact is attested to the commit
# it came from.
desktop/dist/
desktop/vendor/
typescript/**/dist/
typescript/**/build/
typescript/**/.next/
typescript/**/out/
typescript/**/npm-debug.log*
typescript/**/yarn-error.log*

# Go
go/**/*.test
go/**/*.out

# IDE / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Secrets / local config (NEVER commit)
.env
.env.*
*.key
*.pem
secrets/

# Build outputs
*.log
coverage/
.coverage
htmlcov/
.pytest_cache/

# Transient audit virtualenv
.auditvenv/
# Anchored to the repository root on purpose. Unanchored, `evidence/` matches at ANY
# depth -- it silently swallowed rust/evidence/, a real workspace member, so every
# `cargo metadata` in CI failed with 'no such file or directory' and took rustfmt,
# clippy and the conformance matrix down with it.
/evidence/

# Agent hook state -- per-session scratch containing local absolute paths.
# Never useful to a contributor and noise in a public repo.
.mimosa/
**/.mimosa/

# TypeScript incremental build info
*.tsbuildinfo

# ML artifacts (ml/ + python/warrantor_ml). Model weights and corpora must never enter git.
# Root-anchored where a bare directory name would otherwise match at any depth -- the same
# discipline /evidence/ needed after an unanchored `evidence/` swallowed rust/evidence/.
*.safetensors
*.gguf
*.bin
*.pt
*.pth
*.ckpt
*.onnx
*.parquet
checkpoints/
adapter_model/
/ml/data/
/ml/artifacts/
/artifacts/
# Raw per-sample model responses from a benchmark run: ~1.8 MB for a full split, and every
# `--out` example in ml/README.md points here. The commit that added the benchmarks deliberately
# kept these out of git but never actually ignored them, so the next `git add -A` would have
# committed them.
/python/warrantor_ml/eval_sets/results/
# Built training corpora and their manifests. A corpus is reproducible from its manifest plus
# the recipe digest, and committing tens of thousands of rows would redistribute WildGuardMix
# and ExpGuardMix -- which neither corpus's click-through terms permit.
/python/warrantor_ml/corpora/
# ...and at the repository root, which is where ml/README.md's own `--out corpora/weak.jsonl`
# example actually lands when run from the checkout root as documented. Ignoring only the
# package-relative path left the documented command one `git add -A` from committing 28 MB of
# WildGuardMix rows. Same anchoring lesson as /evidence/ and eval_sets/results/, third time.
/corpora/
# Adapters pulled off the Modal volume plus the GGUF and Modelfile that ml/publish_adapter.py
# builds from them. `*.safetensors` and `*.gguf` already catch the weights, but not the
# adapter_config.json, the tokenizer or the Modelfile beside them -- and a partial adapter in
# git is worse than none, because it looks reconstructable and is not.
/adapters/
# Cross-language interop fixtures, regenerated by tools/ci/run_python_checks.py before every test
# run. They land at the repository root and each carries a throwaway signing keypair, so they are
# one `git add -A` from being committed -- which is how a test keypair ends up in history.
.notary_interop_bundle.json
.evidence_interop.json
.bounds_interop.json
